 3. Scoop: &quot;OG&quot; Bill Gates wages war on AI risks 
 
 By Bradley Olson 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Martin Bertrand/Hans Lucas/FP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Bill Gates is gearing up to dramatically expand his AI safety push, personally leaning on business leaders and considering a major deployment of his resources, sources tell Axios.

 Why it matters: The formidable, sharp-elbowed Bill Gates who built Microsoft is back, and he's directing his competitive fire toward what he has come to see as one of the most important causes of his lifetime.

 "I can reach CEOs on this better than you can," Gates told one person he consulted on AI safety, showing an eagerness for combat that associates say is a return to "OG" form. Driving the news: Once a voice for optimism on AI, Gates has grown dismayed by an escalation of model capabilities, alarming security incidents and what he sees as insufficient regulation, sources say.

 As he prepared to make the case for a far more robust regulatory framework on AI, he conferred extensively with leading AI executives, researchers and safety advocates, including Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei. Behind the scenes: People Gates has spoken to called his advocacy a "comeback tour," an attempt to rebuild a legacy that has been tarnished by his past association with Jeffrey Epstein.

 Gates has said he's aware that some people may dismiss his warnings due to the Epstein ties, but sources say he feels the AI moment is too important to stay on the sidelines. The bottom line: As Gates' plans crystallize, it remains to be seen whether AI safety becomes a defining battle of his late career.

